# Grade 12 (part c): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'12-7-1': dict(
  saint="Joseph: tradition says he died with Jesus and Mary at his side, which is why he is patron of a happy death.",
  tryit=["Explain death, particular judgment and general judgment.", "Make a list of what you would want to have done.", "Write a short act of trust."],
  saintStory="The Gospels do not tell us how Joseph died. He is last seen when Jesus is twelve, in the Temple. By the time Jesus begins his public ministry, Joseph is no longer mentioned, and Mary is called a widow in later Christian tradition. Early Christian writings, such as the apocryphal History of Joseph the Carpenter, describe his death in old age, with Jesus and Mary beside him, comforting him.\nThat picture, whether a pious story or a deep intuition, shaped how the Church thinks of Joseph. Christians have long prayed to him for a happy death: a death in the state of grace, comforted by the sacraments, with Jesus and Mary near. Many churches have an altar to Joseph, and confraternities of the “happy death” were formed.\nJoseph’s whole life was a preparation: he listened to God’s messengers in dreams, took Mary home, protected Jesus in danger, worked with his hands, and said nothing in the Gospels. The Church asks us to live as he did, so that we may die as he did.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you knew you had one year to live, what would you change? If you knew you had one day?” Take answers quietly. “Christians don’t avoid the thought of death. They are asked to prepare for it.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism teaches what the Church calls the ‘last things’ or eschatology: death, judgment, heaven, hell, and the end of the world. Death: the separation of the soul from the body. The body decays, the soul, which is immortal, goes to meet God. Death is a consequence of the fall (Romans 5:12), but Christ transformed it. ‘Our Savior Jesus Christ… abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel.’ (2 Timothy 1:10) For the Christian, death is a passage. St. Paul: ‘For me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.’ (Philippians 1:21) Death ends the time of merit; we have only one life (Hebrews 9:27: ‘It is appointed for men to die once, and after that comes judgment’). So the Church rejects reincarnation.”", "Say: “Judgment. The particular judgment: immediately at death, each soul receives its eternal retribution according to its faith and works (CCC 1021-1022). Luke 16 (Lazarus and the rich man), Luke 23:43 (the good thief: ‘today you will be with me in Paradise’). The destination: heaven (immediately or after purification), or hell. The general (final) judgment: at the end of time, Christ will come in glory, the dead will rise, and the whole truth of each life will be revealed. Matthew 25:31-46. The standard: love; ‘I was hungry and you gave me food.’ We will be judged on deeds of mercy. Also on faith (John 3:18). Judgment is a revelation of the truth and an act of justice and mercy.”", "Say: “How should we live in light of this? The Church has a tradition of the memento mori: remembering death. Psalm 90:12: ‘So teach us to number our days that we may get a heart of wisdom.’ Not morbidly, but to focus on what matters. Practical: live in the state of grace; go to Confession regularly; receive the Eucharist; do works of mercy; forgive; make a will; tell loved ones you love them; and pray for a happy death, the Hail Mary: ‘now and at the hour of our death.’ The Church has rites for the dying: Anointing, Viaticum, Commendation. A happy death is a gift: St. Joseph is invoked. The particular and general judgment express that our choices matter. Fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom, but ‘perfect love casts out fear’ (1 John 4:18).”"],
    ask=["What is the particular judgment? (Each soul’s judgment at death.)", "What is the general judgment? (At the end of time, when Christ returns.)", "Why does the Church reject reincarnation? (We die once, and then comes judgment.)"],
    practice=["Draw a line from birth to death, and mark what matters most: relationships, faith, service.", "Write a list of five things you would want to have done by the end of your life.", "Choose one to begin this month.", "Write a short act of trust: “Lord, into your hands I commend my spirit.”"],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary slowly, especially the final line. Add: “St. Joseph, patron of a happy death, pray for us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for a happy death daily.”")),

'12-7-2': dict(
  saint="Catherine of Genoa: a married laywoman who wrote that purgatory is the fire of love.",
  tryit=["Compare heaven, purgatory and hell.", "Match each to Scripture and the Catechism.", "State what the Church leaves undefined."],
  saintStory="Caterina Fieschi was born in 1447 in Genoa, Italy, to a noble family. She wanted to enter a convent, but at sixteen, for political reasons, her family arranged her marriage to Giuliano Adorno, a wild young nobleman who was unfaithful and wasted her dowry on gambling and debt. For about ten years, she was miserable, and turned to worldly amusements.\nOn March 20, 1473, she went to confession in a church and was suddenly overwhelmed by a deep sense of God’s love and of her own sinfulness. She cried, “No more world! No more sin!” From then on, she devoted herself to prayer and service. She and Giuliano, who also changed, agreed to live as brother and sister, and both served the sick at the Pammatone Hospital in Genoa. When plague struck in 1493, she nursed the victims and nearly died herself.\nHer spiritual writings, particularly her Treatise on Purgatory, describe the soul after death as drawn irresistibly to God, and yet ashamed, so that it wants to be purified by love. She died in 1510.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write three words on the board: Heaven, Purgatory, Hell. Ask, “Which of these do people today find hardest to believe? Why?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Heaven. The Catechism: ‘Those who die in God’s grace and friendship and are perfectly purified live for ever with Christ. They are like God for ever, for they ‘see him as he is’, face to face.’ (CCC 1023) This is the beatific vision: seeing God directly. 1 Corinthians 13:12: ‘Now we see in a mirror dimly, but then face to face.’ Revelation 21:4: ‘He will wipe away every tear from their eyes.’ Heaven is communion with the Trinity, Mary, the angels and saints; fulfillment of every good desire; not boredom but joy beyond imagination: ‘What no eye has seen, nor ear heard… God has prepared for those who love him.’ (1 Corinthians 2:9) It includes the resurrection of the body at the end. The saints in heaven pray for us.”", "Say: “Purgatory (see Grade 11). Catherine of Genoa taught that it’s not primarily a place of punishment but a process of purification by God’s love. The souls there are certain of heaven and joyful, but suffer from the delay. They can’t help themselves; we can help them by prayer and sacrifice. Hell. The Catechism: ‘To die in mortal sin without repenting and accepting God’s merciful love means remaining separated from him for ever by our own free choice. This state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God and the blessed is called ‘hell.’’ (CCC 1033) Hell is a real possibility (Matthew 25:41, 46; Mark 9:43-48). The Church teaches that God does not predestine anyone to hell; a person chooses it by rejecting love until the end. The main pain is the loss of God.”", "Say: “Questions. ‘How could a loving God send people to hell?’ He doesn’t send; we choose. C. S. Lewis: ‘The doors of hell are locked on the inside.’ ‘Is anyone in hell?’ The Church has never declared that any person is in hell, not even Judas, but warns it is possible. The Church canonizes saints but not the damned. ‘Is hell eternal?’ Yes, the Church teaches that its punishment is eternal (CCC 1035). ‘What about those who never heard of Christ?’ God’s mercy can reach them (see Grade 10). Apparitions at Fatima describe a vision of hell, as a call to prayer. Our response is to take it seriously, to pray for sinners, and to trust in God’s mercy. Pope Benedict XVI’s Spe Salvi (45-47) says that in most people ‘there remains in the depths of their being an ultimate interior openness to truth, to love, to God,’ and that purification is needed on the way to him.”"],
    ask=["What is the beatific vision? (Seeing God face to face.)", "What is hell, according to the Catechism? (Definitive self-exclusion from communion with God.)", "Has the Church declared anyone to be in hell? (No.)"],
    practice=["Make a three-column chart: Heaven / Purgatory / Hell with key texts, definition, and common misconceptions.", "Write a response to someone who says ‘a loving God wouldn’t allow hell.’", "List what we can do for the souls in purgatory.", "Compose a short prayer for the dead."],
    close="Pray: “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Offer a Mass or a decade for the souls in purgatory.”")),

'12-7-3': dict(
  saint="Gregory of Nyssa: a bishop who taught that heaven is endless growth into God.",
  tryit=["Describe the new creation using Scripture.", "Explain the resurrection of the body.", "Write what you hope for."],
  saintStory="Gregory of Nyssa, born around 335, was the younger brother of Basil the Great and Macrina. He was trained as a teacher of rhetoric, and was married. Basil persuaded him to become bishop of Nyssa, a small town, in 372. He was a gentle man, not suited to church politics, and was falsely accused of embezzlement by supporters of Arian emperor Valens and driven out in 376, but returned in 378 after the emperor’s death.\nAt the Council of Constantinople in 381, he was recognized as a pillar of the faith, and he helped to shape the Nicene Creed. He wrote on the Trinity, the soul, the resurrection, and the spiritual life. In his Life of Moses, he described the spiritual life as an endless journey. Moses, he said, always went further into the darkness of God. This is called epektasis: “stretching forward.” Even in heaven, the soul never exhausts God, but grows forever in love and knowledge.\nHe died around 395. His brother Basil built the Church’s institutions; Gregory contemplated its depths.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Do you imagine heaven as endless sitting on clouds? What would make it truly a place of joy?” Take answers. “The Church has a much richer picture.”",
    teach=["Say: “The last chapters of the Bible, Revelation 21-22, describe a new creation: ‘Then I saw a new heaven and a new earth; for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away.’ The holy city, the new Jerusalem, comes down from God. ‘Behold, the dwelling of God is with men.’ Isaiah 65:17 and 2 Peter 3:13 also speak of ‘new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells.’ The Church teaches that creation itself will be renewed: the material world is not discarded but transformed (Romans 8:19-23: creation groans, waiting for liberation). Heaven isn’t a disembodied existence, but the fulfillment of all things in Christ.”", "Say: “The resurrection of the body. The Creed: ‘I believe in the resurrection of the body and life everlasting.’ At the last day, Christ will raise the dead; our bodies will be reunited with our souls, glorified (1 Corinthians 15:35-53). The resurrected body is the same body, yet transformed, ‘imperishable,’ ‘spiritual.’ Jesus’ risen body is the model: it could be touched, eat fish, yet pass through doors. The Church teaches that all will rise, the just and the unjust (John 5:28-29). This means that the body has dignity. It is why the Church honors the bodies of the dead and prefers burial. It rejects both pure materialism (death is the end) and the notion that the soul alone matters (Gnosticism, reincarnation).”", "Say: “What will heaven be like? We don’t know in detail, but the images: a wedding feast (Revelation 19:9), a city of light, a garden, perfect friendship, rest and work, worship, learning. The Church teaches that we will recognize loved ones; we will be perfected in our individuality. Gregory’s insight: because God is infinite, our growth in him never ends. The joy increases forever. Hope for the new creation changes how we live now: we work for justice and care for creation because they anticipate the Kingdom (Vatican II, GS 39: the good fruits of human work will be found again, purified, in the new earth). We pray ‘Thy kingdom come.’ We live in hope: ‘Maranatha, Come, Lord Jesus.’ The Catechism ends with ‘Amen’: so be it.”"],
    ask=["What does Revelation 21 describe? (A new heaven, new earth and the holy city.)", "What does the Church teach about the resurrection of the body? (We will rise with glorified bodies at the last day.)", "What is epektasis? (Endless growth into God.)"],
    practice=["Draw a picture or write a description of the new creation using only images from Scripture.", "Explain the resurrection of the body in two sentences for a friend.", "List three things you do now that anticipate the Kingdom.", "Write a short prayer of hope."],
    close="Pray: “Come, Lord Jesus.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read Revelation 21:1-7 aloud.”")),

'12-8-1': dict(
  saint="Blessed Ceferino Giménez Malla: a Roma horse-trader and layman who was shot rather than give up his rosary.",
  tryit=["Describe a lay apostolate you could do.", "Match it to a gift and a need.", "Write a one-year plan."],
  saintStory="Ceferino Giménez Malla, nicknamed El Pelé, was born in 1861 in Catalonia, Spain, to a Roma (Gypsy) family. He grew up traveling and became a successful horse trader. He was married in a Catholic ceremony to Teresa Giménez Castro, and they had no children, but adopted a niece. He could not read or write, but was known for honesty, generosity to the poor and love for the faith.\nHe went to Mass often, prayed the Rosary daily, and was a member of the Conference of St. Vincent de Paul. He helped settle disputes among his people and acted as a bridge between the Roma and others. He once helped a priest who was being attacked by a mob and was in turn arrested for defending him.\nIn July 1936, at the start of the Spanish Civil War, anti-Church violence swept Barbastro. Ceferino was arrested for protecting a priest and for carrying a rosary. He was offered release if he gave up his rosary. He refused. On August 9, 1936, he was shot, crying “Long live Christ the King!” He was seventy-five. In 1997, John Paul II beatified him, the first Roma person to be beatified.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you hear ‘mission,’ do you picture a priest in another country? What would be a mission for a high school senior or a nurse or a bus driver?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus’ great commission was given to all the baptized. Vatican II’s Apostolicam Actuositatem (1965) is the decree on the lay apostolate: ‘The Christian vocation is, of its very nature, a vocation to the apostolate.’ The laity share in the Church’s mission by virtue of Baptism and Confirmation. Their field is the world: the temporal order. ‘By their very vocation, lay people seek the kingdom of God by engaging in temporal affairs and directing them according to the plan of God.’ (LG 31) The call to holiness and the call to mission are the same call. John Paul II’s Christifideles Laici (1988) calls the laity to a new evangelization: ‘Go into my vineyard too.’”", "Say: “Forms of lay mission. Witness of life: family, work, friendships; living the Gospel visibly. Evangelization: sharing the faith by word. Service: works of mercy, charity, volunteering. Social and political engagement: justice, advocacy, public life. Ministry within the Church: catechists, readers, music, youth leaders, parish councils, campus ministry. Professional apostolate: doctors, lawyers, teachers, artists. Movements: Catholic Action, St. Vincent de Paul Society, Knights of Columbus, Focolare, Communion and Liberation, Opus Dei, Neocatechumenal Way, Catholic Worker, FOCUS, NET, Life Teen, Young Catholic Professionals. Each person has charisms (1 Corinthians 12). Find yours.”", "Say: “How to start. Pray: discern where God calls. Learn: know your faith. Join: a parish group, a campus ministry, a service organization. Serve: begin small and local. Invite: a friend to Mass, a retreat or an event. Be consistent: mission is a way of life. Obstacles: fear, shyness, busyness, discouragement. The Spirit gives courage. Pope Francis: ‘I am a mission on this earth; that is the reason why I am here in this world.’ (Evangelii Gaudium 273) Ceferino had no formal education or high status, yet he lived his faith by honesty, prayer and courage. He bore witness to Christ among both his own people and others. A plan for next year: choose one way to serve, one way to grow, and one way to invite.”"],
    ask=["What is the lay apostolate? (The laity’s share in the Church’s mission.)", "What is the laity’s special field? (The world: family, work, culture, politics.)", "Name three forms of lay mission. (Witness, service, ministry, social engagement.)"],
    practice=["Make a mission plan: one way to serve, one way to grow, one way to invite.", "For each, write who, what, when.", "Identify a group you can join: parish, campus, service organization.", "Share your plan with a partner who will check in."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, send me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Take the first step in your mission plan this week.”")),

'12-8-2': dict(
  saint="Venerable Madeleine Delbrêl: an atheist teenager who became a social worker and a mystic in a Communist suburb of Paris.",
  tryit=["Design a daily rhythm of prayer.", "Include a time in the city.", "Choose one practice to start."],
  saintStory="Madeleine Delbrêl was born in 1904 in France. As a teenager, she was a brilliant, lively atheist. She wrote at seventeen, “God is dead… long live death.” But she was a sincere seeker. When her friend and fiancé Jean entered the Dominicans, she was shaken, and began, at twenty, to study seriously the faith of her friends. One night, in 1924, she knelt to pray. She wrote that she discovered God exists, and that she wanted to live for him: “God is alive.”\nShe trained as a social worker and in 1933 moved with two friends to Ivry-sur-Seine, a working-class suburb of Paris run by the Communist Party. They formed a small community of lay women, la Charité de Jésus, living the Gospel in the streets. She worked as a municipal social worker and spent time with workers, Communists and the poor. She prayed hours each day, joining contemplation and action.\nShe wrote about finding God in daily life: “The street is our cloister.” She died in 1964. In 2018 she was declared Venerable.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How do you pray when you’re rushed? In class? On the bus? Is that real prayer?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Prayer is the lifeblood of mission. Without it, work becomes activism, and mission dry. Jesus himself went off to pray (Mark 1:35), spent nights in prayer before choosing the Twelve (Luke 6:12), and prayed at Gethsemane. The Church’s tradition of prayer includes the Liturgy: Mass and the Liturgy of the Hours; personal prayer; popular devotions. The Catechism says prayer is ‘the habit of being in the presence of the thrice-holy God and in communion with him.’ (CCC 2565) We pray ‘at all times’ (Ephesians 6:18). A person of prayer sees the world differently. The Church distinguishes the active and contemplative life, but both are needed. Mary and Martha: Jesus said, ‘Mary has chosen the good portion’ (Luke 10:42), not to dismiss service but to say it must flow from listening.”", "Say: “Madeleine Delbrêl lived prayer in the city. She wrote, ‘We, the ordinary people of the streets.’ She said that a person can pray anywhere by offering each task, by short prayers, and by seeing Christ in others. She wrote a ‘Rule of life’ including: a morning offering; the Gospel read daily; silence; the Mass; the Our Father said slowly. A practical daily rhythm: Morning: offering and a short Scripture. Midday: Angelus or pause. Evening: examination of conscience and gratitude. Night: Compline or the Our Father. Weekly: Sunday Mass; Confession monthly; time before the Blessed Sacrament. Seasonal: retreats, days of recollection, Lent and Advent practices. Pray with others: a prayer group, Bible study, family prayer.”", "Say: “Prayer for life: asking God’s help for our needs and for others. The Church’s teaching on intercession: Jesus invites us to ask (Matthew 7:7-11; John 16:23-24). God knows our needs, but wants us to ask; prayer changes us, aligns our will, and God may use our prayer to accomplish good. Unanswered prayer: sometimes God says no or wait; Paul’s thorn (2 Corinthians 12:8-9). Jesus in Gethsemane: ‘Not my will, but yours.’ A prayer for life includes: praying for the sick, the dying, the unborn, for vocations, for peace, and for the Church. The Rosary is a prayer for life. The Church also prays with the great prayers: the Our Father (the model), the Hail Mary, the Creed, and the Psalms. Let prayer be a lifeline of your mission.”"],
    ask=["What does the Catechism say prayer is? (Being in God’s presence and communion with him.)", "What does ‘the street is our cloister’ mean? (We can find God in daily work and city life.)", "Why pray for others? (Jesus invites us, and God uses our prayer.)"],
    practice=["Design your own rule of prayer: a morning, midday, evening and weekly pattern.", "Choose one practice that you will commit to for 30 days.", "Identify an obstacle and a plan to handle it.", "Share with an accountability partner."],
    close="Pray the Our Father slowly, line by line. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Try your prayer rule for a month.”")),

'12-8-3': dict(
  saint="Saint Lorenzo Ruiz: a married Filipino layman and father of three who died in Nagasaki rather than renounce his faith.",
  tryit=["Write your own sending prayer.", "Name where you will be sent.", "Pick a symbol of your mission."],
  saintStory="Lorenzo Ruiz was born around 1600 in Manila, to a Chinese father and Tagalog mother, both Catholics. He was educated by the Dominicans in Binondo, served as an altar boy and sacristan, and became a skilled calligrapher, copying documents for the Dominicans. He married and had two sons and a daughter. He was devout and joined the Confraternity of the Rosary.\nIn 1636, he was falsely accused of a crime, perhaps of a murder, and, in danger, he took refuge on a ship with three Dominican priests who were sailing to Japan, where Christianity was banned. They landed at Okinawa, where they were arrested and sent to Nagasaki.\nThe authorities tortured them, with water forced into the mouth and bamboo under the fingernails, to make them renounce the faith. Lorenzo wavered for a moment, then declared, “I am a Catholic, and I will die for God; if I had a thousand lives, all would be offered to Him.” He was hung upside down in a pit with a stone on his chest, and died on September 29, 1637. He was canonized in 1987, the first Filipino saint.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is the last thing you want to hear at the end of Mass? ‘Go in peace’? Why do you think the Mass ends with a command?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Mass ends with a dismissal: ‘Go forth, the Mass is ended,’ or ‘Go and announce the Gospel of the Lord.’ In Latin, ‘Ite, missa est’; the word ‘Mass’ comes from missa, ‘sent.’ The liturgy culminates in mission. We are sent to bring Christ to the world. The Church’s own nature is missionary (Ad Gentes 2): ‘The pilgrim Church is missionary by her very nature.’ Pope Francis in Evangelii Gaudium: ‘In virtue of their baptism, all the members of the People of God have become missionary disciples.’ The same Christ who calls us, sends us.”", "Say: “Sending in Scripture. Isaiah: ‘Whom shall I send?… Here am I! Send me.’ Jeremiah, Jonah, Mary, the apostles. The Twelve and then seventy-two sent out in pairs (Luke 10:1-12) with little baggage. After the Resurrection: ‘As the Father has sent me, even so I send you.’ (John 20:21) The Great Commission (Matthew 28:19-20) ends with the promise: ‘I am with you always.’ Confirmation: we receive the Spirit to witness. The Church has a long history of sending: the Apostles; Patrick, Boniface, Cyril and Methodius; Francis Xavier; the Maryknoll missionaries. Catholic missions today: Catholic Relief Services, Missionary Societies, lay missionaries, Mission Co-op Sundays, World Mission Sunday. Each parish is a base for mission.”", "Say: “A final reflection. Your faith journey doesn’t end with graduation from Catholic school or religious education. You are being sent into college, work, military, family, friendships. Questions to carry: Will I go to Mass on Sunday? Will I find a community? Will I keep praying? Will I continue to learn? Will I serve? Will I invite? Will I be honest and loving? Will I come back to Confession when I fall? Practical: find a campus ministry or young adult group, a spiritual mentor, a Bible study, a service project. Mission isn’t about perfection but fidelity. Lorenzo Ruiz did not begin as a hero; he was a husband and father who found himself tested. At the dismissal, we’re told: ‘Go in peace, glorifying the Lord by your life.’”"],
    ask=["What does ‘Mass’ come from? (Missa, ‘sent.’)", "What did Jesus say at the end of Matthew’s Gospel? (I am with you always.)", "What can you do after graduation to stay in the faith? (Find community, pray, serve, go to Mass and Confession.)"],
    practice=["Write your own sending prayer: ‘Lord, I am sent to…’", "Write the three places you will be sent in the next year, and one commitment for each.", "Choose a symbol or object to remind you (a cross, a medal, a verse).", "Share one commitment aloud."],
    close="Pray together the dismissal: “Go in peace, glorifying the Lord by your life.” Answer: “Thanks be to God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find a Catholic community at your next school or workplace.”")),

'12-9-1': dict(
  saint="The Twelve Apostles: an old tradition says each contributed one article of the Creed.",
  tryit=["Explain each article of the Creed in one sentence.", "Link each to a Scripture text.", "Pick the one that matters most to you."],
  saintStory="Around the year 400, a priest named Rufinus of Aquileia wrote a commentary on the Apostles’ Creed. He passed on an old tradition: after Pentecost, the apostles were about to scatter in mission. Before leaving Jerusalem, they met to agree on what they would all teach, a “rule of faith,” so that they would not preach different things. Led by the Holy Spirit, each contributed an article.\nLater versions of the story assign the articles to the apostles by name. Peter: “I believe in God the Father almighty, creator of heaven and earth.” Andrew: “and in Jesus Christ, his only Son, our Lord.” James: “who was conceived by the Holy Spirit, born of the Virgin Mary.” John: “suffered under Pontius Pilate, was crucified, died and was buried.” And so on to Matthias: “life everlasting.”\nHistorians doubt that the apostles composed the Creed in this way. The Creed grew out of baptismal professions in Rome over the first centuries. But the legend expresses a truth: the Creed faithfully sums up what the apostles taught. “This is the faith of the Church,” the Catechism says, “and it is the faith of all.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask the class to stand and say the Apostles’ Creed together from memory, as best they can. Then ask, “Which line came out strongest? Which line was hardest to say? Which line do you most want to understand better?” Write the answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Review. The Creed has three parts, as in Baptism: I believe in God the Father, in Jesus Christ the Son, and in the Holy Spirit. First, the Father: God is one, almighty, Creator. The universe is a gift; humans are made in his image; evil entered through sin, not God’s will. Second, Jesus Christ: eternal Son, born of the Virgin Mary by the Holy Spirit, truly God and truly man; suffered and died under Pontius Pilate to save us from sin; descended to the dead; rose on the third day; ascended to the Father; will come to judge. Third, the Holy Spirit, the Lord and giver of life, who inspired the prophets, dwells in the Church; the one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church; the communion of saints; the forgiveness of sins; the resurrection of the body; life everlasting.”", "Say: “Sorting the Creed into central points. The Trinity. The Incarnation. The Paschal Mystery (Passion, death, Resurrection). The Church. The last things. Each connects to the others. Reviewing the story: Creation, fall, covenant, Christ, Church, new creation. The Creed is the story of salvation in short. Also, review how we know: Scripture, Tradition and Magisterium; faith and reason; reading the Bible in context; the councils (Nicaea, Constantinople, Ephesus, Chalcedon) that shaped the Creed. Also: Mary (Immaculate Conception, Mother of God, Assumption). The Creed is not just a list but a relationship: ‘I believe in’ means trust in a Person, not just assent to propositions.”", "Say: “The Creed and life. Every article has consequences. God the Father: trust, gratitude, stewardship. Jesus the Son: discipleship, imitation, hope. The Holy Spirit: prayer, gifts, mission. The Church: belonging, obedience, service. Communion of saints: friendship with the saints and prayer for the dead. Forgiveness: Confession. Resurrection: hope, dignity of the body. Life everlasting: meaning in suffering and death. As you prepare your capstone statement of faith, think about how each part of the Creed shapes how you live. The Creed was a living confession for martyrs. It isn’t finished once you say it; it’s a life to be lived. ‘I believe; help my unbelief.’”"],
    ask=["What are the three parts of the Creed? (Father, Son, Holy Spirit.)", "Which council wrote the Nicene Creed? (Nicaea and Constantinople.)", "What does ‘I believe in’ mean? (Trust in a Person, not only agreement with statements.)"],
    practice=["Write each article of the Apostles’ Creed on a card.", "On the back, write one Scripture text and one sentence of explanation.", "Rank the articles by how clear they are to you, and mark the two you want to study more.", "Share your list with a partner and trade explanations."],
    close="Pray the Apostles’ Creed together, slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Write your own one-sentence version of each part of the Creed.”")),

'12-9-2': dict(
  saint="John Baptist de La Salle: a priest who gave away his fortune to start schools and taught teachers how to teach the faith.",
  tryit=["Make a one-page map of the sacraments and morals.", "Connect each sacrament to a virtue and a commandment.", "Identify your gaps."],
  saintStory="John Baptist de La Salle was born in 1651 in Reims, France, the oldest of ten children of a wealthy family. He studied for the priesthood and became a canon of the cathedral at twenty-seven. In 1679, he met a man named Adrien Nyel, who was starting free schools for poor boys. At first, John only helped, but he saw that the teachers were poorly trained, and he began to take them into his home and train them.\nGradually he gave himself fully. He resigned his canonry, and in 1684 distributed his wealth to the poor during a famine. He founded the Brothers of the Christian Schools, a community of lay teachers. He developed new methods: teaching in French instead of Latin, grouping students by ability, teaching in classes rather than one by one, and including religion. He wrote the Conduct of Schools.\nHe died in 1719. In 1900 he was canonized, and in 1950 named patron of teachers. His life shows that clear teaching is a work of love.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you had to teach the Catholic faith to a younger student in five minutes, what would you cover? What would you leave out? How would you check they understood?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Review of the sacraments. Initiation: Baptism (new birth, forgiveness, incorporation), Confirmation (the Spirit’s seal for witness), Eucharist (source and summit; sacrifice, real presence, communion). Healing: Reconciliation (contrition, confession, satisfaction, absolution), Anointing (strength in illness). Service: Holy Orders (bishop, priest, deacon; acting in persona Christi), Matrimony (covenant; spouses are ministers; unity, indissolubility, openness to life). Each has matter, form, minister and effect. Which can be received only once: Baptism, Confirmation, Orders (they imprint a character). How they work: ex opere operato, with the disposition of the recipient. Who is the minister of each? Know the answers.”", "Say: “Review of morals. The foundation: dignity of the person, made in God’s image; the Beatitudes as our end; freedom and responsibility; the three sources of morality (object, intention, circumstances); conscience formed by the Church’s teaching; law (eternal, natural, Old, New); sin (mortal and venial; the capital sins); grace and the virtues (theological and cardinal). The Ten Commandments: love of God (no other gods; the name; the Lord’s Day) and neighbor (family; life; chastity; justice; truth; purity of heart; detachment). Catholic social teaching: dignity, common good, subsidiarity, solidarity, the option for the poor, stewardship of creation. Apply to cases: life, marriage and sexuality, justice, war and peace.”", "Say: “Study methods for review. Use the Catechism of the Catholic Church’s Compendium. Make flashcards. Work in pairs and quiz each other. Make a one-page ‘map’ connecting sacraments, virtues and commandments. Explain a topic to a younger student; teaching is the best test. Link each doctrine to Scripture and to a saint. Distinguish ‘what the Church teaches’ from opinion. Identify gaps and plan to fill them. De La Salle’s approach: clear, patient, adapted to the student. A Catholic in the world needs to be able to explain. Pope Francis: ‘Be prepared… be brave.’ Your capstone is a chance to show this.”"],
    ask=["Which three sacraments leave a character? (Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders.)", "What are the three sources of morality? (Object, intention, circumstances.)", "What are the principles of Catholic social teaching? (Dignity, common good, subsidiarity, solidarity, option for the poor, stewardship.)"],
    practice=["Draw a one-page map with the seven sacraments at the center, connected to virtues, commandments and Scripture.", "Mark with a star the three items you know best and a question mark on the three you know least.", "Quiz a partner with five questions each.", "Plan how to fill your gaps before the capstone."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make me a clear and faithful witness.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Teach one topic to a family member.”")),

'12-9-3': dict(
  saint="Paul at Miletus: “I have finished the race.”",
  tryit=["Outline your statement of faith.", "Add a story and a Scripture.", "Rehearse a three-minute version."],
  saintStory="Near the end of his third missionary journey, Paul wanted to hurry to Jerusalem. He stopped at Miletus, a port near Ephesus, and sent for the elders of the Ephesian church to meet him. His farewell to them in Acts 20 is the only speech of Paul to Christians in the Acts.\nHe reminded them of how he had lived among them for three years, serving the Lord with humility and tears, teaching in public and from house to house. He told them he was going to Jerusalem, “constrained by the Spirit,” not knowing what would happen, except that “in every city the Holy Spirit testifies to me that imprisonment and afflictions await me.” He added, “But I do not account my life of any value nor as precious to myself, if only I may accomplish my course and the ministry which I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify to the gospel of the grace of God.”\nHe warned them to guard the flock, and entrusted them to God. They wept, embraced him and kissed him, grieving most that they would not see him again. Years later, from prison in Rome, he wrote: “I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.” (2 Timothy 4:7)",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “You’re about to give your statement of faith. Take a moment to think: if a friend asked, ‘What do you believe, and why does it matter to you?’, what would you want to be able to say in three minutes?” Let students write silently for two minutes.",
    teach=["Say: “A statement of faith is a personal, honest account of what you believe, why and how it shapes your life. It is not a recitation of the Creed, but your own voice in harmony with the Church’s faith. Ancient examples: the confession of Peter (Matthew 16:16); the martyrs’ responses at trial; Augustine’s Confessions; Thérèse’s Story of a Soul; Newman’s Apologia; Paul’s speech at Miletus. Elements: what I believe (a section drawn from the Creed), why I believe (reasons, experiences, witnesses), how I live it (practices, commitments), and what I hope for (the future).”", "Say: “Plan your presentation. One: Opening: a story or question that draws attention. Two: Core beliefs: pick three to five central truths, such as that God is Father, Jesus is Lord, the Church is his Body, the sacraments give grace, I am called to love. Three: Evidence and reasons: Scripture, reason, the lives of saints, personal experience. Four: Challenge: name a doubt or difficulty you have faced, and how you worked through it; being honest makes it credible. Five: Commitment: what you will do, specifically, in the next year. Six: Closing: a prayer or Scripture. Keep it to about three to five minutes. Practice aloud. Be yourself. Use notes if you need them.”", "Say: “Expectations. Be accurate: check the Catechism. Be clear: explain terms. Be personal: speak from your heart. Be respectful: of those who disagree. Be brief. Listeners: listen attentively, respond with encouragement, ask a question, and affirm. Close with a prayer for one another. Remember Paul’s words: ‘I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.’ You haven’t finished the race, but you can say, ‘I press on toward the goal.’ (Philippians 3:14) The faith is a gift to be handed on. Thank those who taught you. Celebrate. This is not the end, but a sending forth.”"],
    ask=["What are the parts of a statement of faith? (What I believe, why, how I live it, what I hope for.)", "How long should the presentation be? (About three to five minutes.)", "What should listeners do? (Listen, encourage, ask a question.)"],
    practice=["Write an outline of your statement: opening, core beliefs, reasons, a difficulty, a commitment, closing.", "Rehearse it aloud to a partner, timing it.", "Revise based on feedback.", "Present to the class (or in small groups) and respond to questions."],
    close="Pray together a prayer of blessing for the class, ending with the Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank someone who passed the faith to you, in writing.”")),
}
